                  {/* Section 4 */}
                  <section id="cibil-impact" className="scroll-mt-32">
                    <h2 className="text-xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 md:mb-6">Impact of Debt Settlement on Your CIBIL Score</h2>
                    <p className="text-sm md:text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4 text-gray-700">
                      While a loan settlement provides immediate financial relief, it has long-term implications for your credit profile. Borrowers must understand these consequences to plan their financial recovery. Lenders report every settlement to credit bureaus, and the way this information is logged will affect your ability to borrow in the future.
                    </p>

                    <h3 className="text-lg md:text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-3 mt-6">The Distinction Between 'Settled' and 'Closed' Tags</h3>
                    <p className="text-sm md:text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4 text-gray-700">
                      When a loan is paid in full, the lender reports it to CIBIL as 'Closed'. This is a positive status showing that you fulfilled your entire financial obligation. If you negotiate a settlement, the bank reports it as 'Settled'. This tag remains on your credit history for seven years, indicating that you did not pay the full amount due.
                    </p>
                    <p className="text-sm md:text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4 text-gray-700">
                      This tag typically leads to a drop in your credit score, and future lenders may view you as a higher-risk borrower. To understand the details, read about how <Link href="/does-loan-settlement-affect-cibil-score" className="text-[#D2A02A] hover:underline font-semibold">does loan settlement affect CIBIL score</Link> and learn about the timeline of <Link href="/loan-settlement-after-npa-classification" className="text-[#D2A02A] hover:underline font-semibold">loan settlement after NPA classification</Link>.
                    </p>

                    <h3 className="text-lg md:text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-3 mt-6">How to Rebuild Your Credit Score After a Settlement</h3>
                    <p className="text-sm md:text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4 text-gray-700">
                      Rebuilding your credit profile after a settlement takes time and discipline. The first step is to review your credit report regularly to ensure all other accounts are reported correctly. To start improving your score, you can apply for a secured credit card backed by a fixed deposit. Making timely payments on this card demonstrates responsible credit behavior.
                    </p>
                    <p className="text-sm md:text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4 text-gray-700">
                      You can also take out small consumer loans and pay them off on time to establish a positive payment history. Over 18 to 24 months, these steps can help you rebuild your credit score. For more advice on managing your credit post-resolution, read about <Link href="/what-happens-after-loan-settlement" className="text-[#D2A02A] hover:underline font-semibold">what happens after loan settlement</Link>.
                    </p>
                  </section>

